trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Social history is now largely complete: no tobacco, minimal alcohol, no recreational drugs, low-risk occupational exposure — making smoking-related malignancy, alcoholic myopathy, and toxic neuropathy less likely (though not impossible). The clinical picture remains concerning for insidious systemic disease: unintentional weight loss + progressive weakness over months, without constitutional symptoms or GI complaints. Next highest-yield domain is targeted physical examination (domain 7), as objective findings would dramatically narrow the differential. For example:  
- Cachexia or temporal muscle wasting suggests chronic illness or malignancy;  
- Lymphadenopathy (especially supraclavicular or generalized) raises concern for lymphoma or metastatic cancer;  
- Thyromegaly or tremor could indicate undiagnosed hyperthyroidism despite patient’s denial;  
- Pallor may point to anemia (e.g., from occult GI bleed or chronic disease);  
- Hepatosplenomegaly suggests hematologic or infiltrative disease;  
- Proximal muscle weakness on exam (e.g., difficulty rising from chair, climbing stairs) would support myopathy (e.g., inflammatory, endocrine, or paraneoplastic); distal weakness would suggest neuropathy.  
Since no physical exam data has been provided yet, and it’s essential before interpreting labs meaningfully, I will request the most informative elements of the exam — vital signs, BMI, general appearance, lymph nodes, thyroid, and neuromuscular assessment — as a single coordinated [TEST] command (as physical exam is performed by clinician, not reported by patient).

[TEST] Perform and report: vital signs (BP, HR, RR, Temp, SpO₂), current weight and BMI, general appearance (e.g., cachexia, pallor, jaundice), thyroid exam (size, nodules, tenderness), lymph node exam (cervical, supraclavicular, axillary, inguinal), and manual muscle testing of proximal muscles (hip flexors, shoulder abductors) bilaterally.

